TSI device tree sources
=======================

tsi-skylp-chiplet.dtsi
    SkyLP chiplet CSR window + IO-corner pinctrl/GPIO nodes (RAL-sourced).
    A fragment, not a standalone board: it is spliced onto the QEMU tsi-fpga
    machine's synthesized base DTB by the TSISIM deployment
    (tsi_yocto scripts/build_bmc_kernel.sh), which dtc-recompiles the result.
    Requires CONFIG_ARCH_TSI + CONFIG_TSI_CHIPLET + CONFIG_PINCTRL_TSI.


Verifying the TSI chiplet / pinctrl / GPIO enablement
=====================================================

Enabling ARCH_TSI + TSI_CHIPLET + PINCTRL_TSI in the arm64 defconfig and
adding the chiplet nodes above is validated two ways, both reproducible.

1. Driver unit tests (KUnit, host-only, no hardware/simulator)

   The pinctrl/GPIO driver ships a KUnit suite that checks pad-to-data-bit
   mapping, iomode/mux encodings, group-wide muxing, direction and pinconf
   encoding, register-span coverage, and per-corner registration against a
   fake register model:

       ./tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py run \
           --kunitconfig drivers/pinctrl/tsi/pinctrl-tsi.kunitconfig

   Expected: "Ran 52 tests: passed: 52" (suite pinctrl-tsi).

   Note: on a host GCC newer than this tree expects, the ARCH=um build may
   trip -Werror=missing-prototypes in unrelated lib/ code. In that case run
   the full command with WERROR disabled (--kconfig_add and its value are
   two separate arguments):

       ./tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py run \
           --kunitconfig drivers/pinctrl/tsi/pinctrl-tsi.kunitconfig \
           --kconfig_add CONFIG_WERROR=n

2. Simulator boot probe (TSISIM functional simulator + vFlex)

   Build and boot the BMC-on-TSISIM environment (see tsi_yocto
   docs/build-flavors.md, flavor bmc-tsisim), then on the guest:

       # dmesg
       tsi-chiplet 2040000000.chiplet: chiplet 0: CSR window [mem 0x2040000000-0x205bffffff]

       # gpiodetect
       gpiochip0 [2053000000.pinctrl] (26 lines)   # IONW (chiplet base + 0x13000000)
       gpiochip1 [2046000000.pinctrl] (25 lines)   # IONE (chiplet base + 0x06000000)
       gpiochip2 [2049000000.pinctrl] (2 lines)    # IOSE (chiplet base + 0x09000000)
       gpiochip3 [2059000000.pinctrl] (12 lines)   # IOSW (chiplet base + 0x19000000)

   Before this change: driver not compiled in, /dev/gpiochip* absent.
   After: tsi-chiplet probes and four gpiochips enumerate, one per corner.

   GPIO runs in polled mode (no "interrupts" in the DT) until the
   collector-to-GIC SPI wiring is confirmed; see the pinctrl binding.
